          %title
          %h1#section_table_of_contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#introduction"} Introduction
              %li
                %a{:href => "#model_structures"} Model structures
              %li
                %a{:href => "#traditional_recipes_for_abelian_sheaf_cohomology"} Traditional recipes for abelian sheaf cohomology
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
          %h2#introduction Introduction
          %p
            The purpose of this article is to explain how the general abstract techniques for computing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+cohomology"} sheaf cohomology
            described in articles like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structures+on+simplicial+presheaves"} model structures on simplicial presheaves
            connect to the more traditional techniques for computing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
            such as injective resolutions, acyclic resolutions, Čech cohomology, Cartan and Leray theorems, etc.
          %h2#model_structures Model structures
          %p
            Fix some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            S such as Zariski, Nisnevich, etc., or one of the differential-geometric or complex-geometric sites. We are interested in the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"} sheaves
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
            on S, and how to compute
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
            on S using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structures"} model structures
            on these categories.
          %p
            The following three
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            are
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalent"} Quillen equivalent
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The local
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+model+structure"} projective model structure
                on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
                of abelian groups.
            %li
              %p
                The local
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+model+structure"} injective model structure
                on chain complexes of presheaves of abelian groups;
            %li
              %p
                The local
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+model+structure"} injective model structure
                on chain complexes of sheaves of abelian groups;
          %p
            For typical sites, it is not possible to construct a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+model+structure"} projective model structure
            or a local projective model structure on chain complexes of sheaves of abelian groups, which is often expressed by saying that the category of sheaves of abelian groups does not have enough
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+objects"} projective objects
          %p
            Here “local” means the same thing in all three cases: the class of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            of these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structures"} model structures
            is precisely the class of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
            of presheaves that induce isomorphisms on sheaves of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"} homology
            groups. The latter can be computed as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+sheaves"} associated sheaves
            of presheaves of homology groups, which are computed objectwise on the site.
          %p
            Furthermore, for a site with enough points (as is the case with the Zariski and Nisnevich site, as well as pretty much any site used in differential or complex geometry), the class of weak equivalences can also be described as the class of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
            of presheaves that at every point of the site induces a quasi-isomorphism of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stalks"} stalks
            at this point.
          %p
            Next, if the site is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypercomplete"} hypercomplete
            (as is the case for any site with enough points), the following
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalent"} Quillen equivalent
            to each other and to the model categories 1-3), in fact we have equalities 1=4, 2=5:
          %p
            4. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+model+structure"} projective model structure
            on chain complexes of presheaves of abelian groups left Bousfield localized at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cech+nerves"} Čech nerves
            of covering families.
          %p
            5. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+model+structure"} injective model structure
            on chain complexes of presheaves of abelian groups left Bousfield localized at Čech nerves of covering families;
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structures"} model structures
            1) and 2) can in general also be expressed as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+Bousfield+localization"} left Bousfield localization
            of the projective/injective model structure on presheaves, this time with respect to the (larger) class of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypercovers"} hypercovers
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypercomplete"} hypercomplete
          %p
            The model structure 3) is typically not a localization of a global model structure, however, it can be easily constructed directly using, e.g., the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Smith+recognition+theorem"} Smith recognition theorem
          %p
            There is an analogous statement in the setting of sheaves of abelian groups: the category of sheaves of abelian groups is the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+fractions"} category of fractions
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+localization"} reflective localization
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+localizations"} reflective localizations
          %p
            To emphasize again: if the site has enough points, all three notions of a local weak equivalence: isomorphisms on homology sheaves, stalkwise quasi-isomorphisms, and Čech-local weak equivalences coincide. They are quite different from ordinary (objectwise) weak equivalences of presheaves, i.e., objectwise
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphisms"} quasi-isomorphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/etale+site"} etale site
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cech+descent"} Čech descent
            from
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperdescent"} hyperdescent
          %p
            The advantage of 4) and 5) is that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+objects"} fibrant objects
            are now easier to describe: they are precisely
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+objects"} fibrant objects
            in the original model structure (before a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+Bousfield+localization"} left Bousfield localization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"} homotopy limit
            of the remaining cosimplicial diagram. This is simply the homotopy coherent version of the usual gluing axiom for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"} sheaves
            of abelian groups.
          %p
            Any presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is weakly equivalent (in the localized model structure) to a local presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(F)
            via a (local) weak equivalence
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo →
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\to L(F)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+sheaf"} associated sheaf
            map. It is important not confuse the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\to L(F)
            with an entirely different map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\to K(F)
            that is given by taking the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+sheaf"} associated sheaf
            of abelian groups of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_n
            separately in every degree
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            in fact gives a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
            2→3, and in general has nothing to do with the functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+replacement"} fibrant replacement
            functor in the model structures 1-5).
          %p
            Indeed, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+replacement"} fibrant replacement
            functor in 1-5) can be computed by applying first
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+replacement+functor"} fibrant replacement functor
            in the original (global) model structure. For
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+model+structures"} projective model structures
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+model+structures"} injective model structures
          %p
            The relevance of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+replacement"} fibrant replacement
            functor is explained by its relevance for computing the derived mapping chain complex of presheaves of chain complexes. Recall that given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            of chain complexes of abelian groups, we can define the mapping chain complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Map
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Map(F,G)
            using the usual formula with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernels"} kernels
            (or
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizers"} equalizers
          %p
            What we really are interested in is the derived mapping chain complex
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi RMap
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo ,
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RMap(F,G)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Map
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Q
                    %mi F
                    %mo ,
                    %mi R
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Map(Q F, R G)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            is a cofibrant replacement functor and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a fibrant replacement functor, in any of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structures"} model structures
            1-5). In the more traditional language, (the cohomology groups of) RMap are also known as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypercohomology"} hypercohomology
            (groups) of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            with coefficients in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            The derived mapping chain complex depends only on the class of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi RMap
                    %mi local
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RMap_{local}(F,G)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi RMap
                    %mi global
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RMap_{global}(F,G)
            to distinguish the cases when we use local weak equivalence versus objectwise weak equivalences.
          %p The fundamental relation between two chain complexes is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi RMap
                    %mi local
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi RMap
                    %mi global
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RMap_{local}(F,G) = RMap_{global}(F,L(G)),
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\to L(G)
            is the localization map described above. In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+model+structure"} injective model structure
            (local or not) all objects are cofibrant, so in this case we can take
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                    %mo =
                    %mi id
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q=id
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+model+structure"} projective model structure
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo =
                    %mi id
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R=id
          %p
            For the local
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+model+structures"} injective model structures
            2, 3, 5 all objects are cofibrant, so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            can be taken to be the identity functor, whereas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is (for example) the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+resolution"} injective resolution
            functor. In these cases, we obtain the fairly traditional recipe for computing
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            of coefficients by its fibrant replacement, e.g., the injective resolution of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          %p
            For the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+model+structures"} projective model structures
            1, 4 fibrancy boils down to locality, i.e., the homotopy coherent descent condition, so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo =
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R=L
            is the homotopy coherent analogue of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+sheaf"} associated sheaf
            functor, whereas cofibrant objects are the same in the local and global model structure and are degreewise
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retracts"} retracts
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
            of representables
          %p
            Now the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
            of an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in the site with coefficients in a sheaf of abelian groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            can be computed as follows. First, convert
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to its representable presheaf of sets, then convert the resulting presheaf of sets into a presheaf of abelian groups by applying the free abelian group functor objectwise. We will abuse the language and continue to refer to such presheaves of abelian groups as representable presheaf.
          %p
            In fact, the same technique continues to work for more general objects than objects of the site, e.g., for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+spaces"} algebraic spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            are presheaves of abelian groups, which we can convert into presheaves of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            by placing them in chain degree 0. The
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+cohomology"} sheaf cohomology
            group of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with coefficients in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is now simply the nth cohomology group of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi RMap
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RMap(X,F)
          %h2#traditional_recipes_for_abelian_sheaf_cohomology Traditional recipes for abelian sheaf cohomology
          %p
            We can now easily recover all traditional recipes for computing
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                In the model structure 1), we can replace F by its
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+resolution"} injective resolution
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+replacement"} fibrant replacement
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                = succeed "th" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                comes from a representable presheaf, then we can simply evaluate the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+resolution"} injective resolution
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                and take its cohomology.
            %li
              %p
                We can also replace
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                by its acyclic resolution
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                via a map
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo →
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\to A
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+object"} fibrant object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi RMap
                        %mi local
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RMap_{local}(X,F)
                can be computed as
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi RMap
                          %mi global
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RMap_{global}(X,A)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is representable, which means that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi RMap
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RMap(X,A)
                can be computed by evaluating
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                on
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+model+structure"} projective model structure
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is cofibrant and any object (including
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+theorem"} de Rham theorem
            %li
              %p
                In the model structure 3) we can fibrantly replace
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                by taking its associated homotopy coherent sheaf
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi L
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(F)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                by taking the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cech+nerve"} Čech nerve
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˇ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \check C(U)
                of its covering family
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi RMap
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RMap(X,F)
                can be computed as
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi RMap
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mover
                          %mi C
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˇ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %mi L
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RMap(\check C(U),L(F))
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi L
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(F)
            %li
              %p
                To recover Cartan’s and Leray’s theorems, first replace
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˇ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \check C(U)
                and compute
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi RMap
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi RMap
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mover
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˇ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi RMap
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi hocolim
                        %mi n
                      %mover
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˇ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi holim
                        %mi n
                      %mi RMap
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mover
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˇ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %msub
